     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 7,650 mg/day of metformin is three times the maximum labeled dose. I know of no clinical situation where that is therapeutic. It puts the patient at serious risk of life-threatening lactic acidosis, and normal renal and hepatic function doesn't reduce that risk enough. A single 2,550 mg dose is also well above the usual maximum for one administration, which is about 1,000 mg.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO TID with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum labeled dose.\n- **Metformin 1000 mg PO BID with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **Any other regimen within labeled limits.**\n\nIf you think there's a real indication for going above the labeled maximum, I'd suggest discussing it with pharmacy first. The order can then go through whatever override or verification process your institution requires."
      }
     ],
